        <span class="pager__halo" data-halo="right" data-no-reveal aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <img loading="lazy" class="pager__layer pager__mt1" data-mt1="dock" data-no-reveal data-critical src="{{ basePath | default('') }}static/image/Animation/index/lab/MT1.png"
          alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="1600" height="1600" decoding="async">


        <!-- 动画不是唯一的信息载体：无障碍与无脚本读法都靠这段文字（含科学表述边界） -->
        <p class="sr-only">How the PAGER receptor is designed to work. The yeast cell membrane is designed to
          carry an hM1Dq receptor whose extracellular recognition unit is an anti-H1N1 HA nanobody (Nb),
          while the MT1 inhibitor is designed to keep hM1Dq blocked whenever no antigen is present. When
          an influenza A (H1N1) HA antigen binds the nanobody, the inhibitor is designed to be displaced
          from the receptor; with DCZ holding hM1Dq ready, the receptor is designed to hand the signal to
          the humanised G&alpha; protein (the <i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i> Gpa1 C-terminus, last five
          residues changed from KIGII to EYNLV) and then to the yeast MAPK cascade, where the <i>FUS1</i> promoter
          drives the <i>yEGFP</i> or <i>lacZ</i> reporter. All of this is the intended design; no detection performance is
          claimed here.</p>

